NFSv3 server tests
==================

This directory contains direct NFSv3 RPC tests for the 22 procedures in
RFC 1813, implemented using the NFSv3 XDR definitions in
`nfs4.1/xdrdef/nfs3.x` and the existing NFSv3 RPC client.

From this directory, generate the XDR modules and run the test suite with:

    (cd .. && python3 setup.py build)
    python3 testserver.py SERVER /export/path

The top-level build must run from the repository root, because it locates
the xdr, rpc, nfs4.1, and nfs4.0 directories relative to its working directory.
The subshell above returns you to nfs3 after the build.

The export must permit the selected AUTH_SYS credentials to create and remove
files and directories. The runner mounts the export through MOUNTv3, creates
a private test directory, runs the procedures, and removes its test objects.
Use `--list` to see all test cases, `--port PORT` to set the NFS port, and
procedure names (for example `READ WRITE COMMIT`) to run each selected
procedure together with its edge cases. A specific case can also be selected
by name (for example `READ_MAX_COUNT`). Edge cases include byte-exact,
non-UTF-8 filenames and symbolic-link targets, as NFSv3 XDR strings are opaque
byte strings and do not require UTF-8 encoding.

Linux nfsd argument validation
-----------------------------

The LINUX_* edge cases exercise request checks in fs/nfsd/nfs3xdr.c and
fs/nfsd/nfs3proc.c and run with the other cases. Select one by name, for
example `LINUX_WRITE_COUNT`. They are Linux implementation checks, not
portable RFC requirements: rejected XDR arguments are expected to produce
RPC GARBAGE_ARGS rather than an NFS status. Raw payloads bypass client-side
XDR validation for oversized handles and invalid enum discriminants.

Coverage: handle lengths and truncation; empty, oversized, NUL, and
slash-containing names across all directory-argument procedures; WRITE
count/data mismatches, invalid stability and truncated data; invalid CREATE
modes and short exclusive verifiers; truncated symlink targets; and invalid
versus valid-but-illegal MKNOD types (the latter return NFS3ERR_BADTYPE).

Source reviewed: Linux revision f66469d9905b39d2c260a15d7d6db450f4db67c1,
https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/torvalds/linux.git/tree/fs/nfsd?id=f66469d9905b39d2c260a15d7d6db450f4db67c1

Offline regression checks, run from the repository root:

    python3 -m unittest nfs3.test_linux_arguments nfs3.test_client rpc.test_connection
